Title:
MostShittyEDR: Building a Terrible EDR to Learn Bypassing

Type:
Blog Post (with companion GitHub Tool / Challenge Lab)

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
MostShittyEDR is an intentionally weak EDR written in Nim, designed as a learning platform for understanding how EDRs work and how attackers bypass them. It implements a dual-mode architecture: a user-mode agent (polling-based monitoring and userland actions) and an optional kernel driver mode using real Windows kernel callbacks for process/thread/image events and LSASS handle protection. The post explains common EDR sensor layers (kernel callbacks, user-mode inline hooks, ETW/Threat Intelligence telemetry) and maps them to nine deliberately bypassable detection rules (blacklists, command-line keywords, PowerShell flags, hash signatures, hook profiles, ETW session checks, PE heuristics). The project includes a structured lab with 42 challenges across 11 categories, ranging from trivial rename/obfuscation to direct syscall evasion, ETW tampering, and BYOVD/IOCTL abuse against the driver. It’s primarily useful for red teamers, pentesters, and malware developers practicing evasion, but also valuable for defenders to understand failure modes and misconfigurations (e.g., weak driver ACLs). The kernel-mode “intentional weaknesses” mirror real-world EDR-killer patterns: unauthenticated IOCTL access, event-queue DoS/blinding, and rule manipulation.

Technical Focus:
- Windows EDR architecture (agent/sensors/telemetry/detection logic)
- Kernel callbacks: PsSetCreateProcessNotifyRoutineEx, PsSetCreateThreadNotifyRoutine, ObRegisterCallbacks
- User-mode inline hooking of ntdll syscall stubs and trampoline forwarding
- ETW and Microsoft-Windows-Threat-Intelligence telemetry and bypass concepts
- BYOVD-style driver abuse and IOCTL interface exploitation
- Detection-rule weaknesses (blacklists, keyword matching, hash-based signatures, PE heuristics)

Use Cases:
- Build hands-on intuition for EDR visibility gaps and bypass primitives
- Practice user-mode evasion: renaming, command-line obfuscation, alternate PowerShell hosts, timing/polling gaps
- Practice advanced tradecraft: direct syscalls / unhooking strategies and hook detection
- Explore ETW session manipulation and telemetry blinding approaches
- Learn kernel attack surfaces: abusing misconfigured device objects/IOCTLs to disable or subvert protections
- Defensive training: identify brittle detection logic and dangerous driver design patterns

Keywords:
EDR, Windows internals, Nim, kernel driver, user-mode hooks, inline hooking, ntdll, syscalls, direct syscalls, ETW, Microsoft-Windows-Threat-Intelligence, PsSetCreateProcessNotifyRoutineEx, PsSetCreateThreadNotifyRoutine, ObRegisterCallbacks, LSASS protection, IOCTL, device object ACL, BYOVD, process creation blocking, PE heuristics, SHA256 signatures, command-line obfuscation, PowerShell evasion